<objective>
Report every Terraform resource block added on this branch together with its import ID, written in the exact format the provider's official documentation specifies for that resource type and provider version.
</objective>

<import_id_format>
This applies to EVERY Terraform provider: AWS, Azure, Google Cloud, OCI, vSphere, Kubernetes, and any other provider the blocks use.

A listed id is how the cloud's inventory API names a resource (an ARN, an Azure resource ID, a GCP asset name, an OCID, a vSphere managed object reference, …). It is NOT necessarily the import ID. Every Terraform resource type documents its own import ID format in the "Import" section of its page on the Terraform Registry, and that format differs from one resource type to the next, even within a single provider. One resource type may want the full listed id, the next only a name or a short id taken from it, another a composite of several of its parts, a path, a URL or a key=value string.

To show how far apart the formats can be, here is a small sample across providers:

| Provider | Resource type | Documented import ID form |
|---|---|---|
| AWS | `aws_s3_bucket` | bucket name, e.g. `acme-logs` (not the ARN) |
| AWS | `aws_vpc` | VPC id, e.g. `vpc-0123` (not the ARN) |
| AWS | `aws_iam_policy` | the full ARN |
| AWS | `aws_ecs_service` | `<cluster>/<service>`, e.g. `prod/api` |
| Azure | `azurerm_storage_account` | the full Azure resource ID |
| Google Cloud | `google_project_iam_member` | `<project> <role> <member>`, space-separated |
| OCI | `oci_core_vcn` | the OCID |
| OCI | `oci_objectstorage_bucket` | `n/<namespace>/b/<bucket_name>` |
| vSphere | `vsphere_virtual_machine` | inventory path, e.g. `/<datacenter>/vm/<folder>/<vm_name>` |
| vSphere | `vsphere_tag` | JSON, `{"category_name": "<category>", "tag_name": "<tag>"}` |
| Kubernetes | `kubernetes_manifest` | `apiVersion=<v>,kind=<kind>,namespace=<ns>,name=<name>` |

This table is NOT a reference and NOT a complete list. It shows a handful of the thousands of resource types that exist, only to make the point that formats differ. Most resource types you meet will not be in it, and formats change between provider versions, so the documentation of the provider version in use is always the authority, for every resource type, including the ones above. Never take a format from this table without reading the documentation.

Building the import ID:

- Take the format from the documentation, and fill it with values present in the listed id: a name, a short id, an OCID, a path, the account, subscription, project, compartment or tenancy, the region, a parent such as a cluster, resource group, namespace, folder or datacenter. Documented literal text around those values (separators, a fixed URL host pattern, a `/`, `,` or space between parts, JSON keys) is part of the format and is written as documented. For example, for `aws_vpc` the listed id `arn:aws:ec2:eu-west-1:111122223333:vpc/vpc-0123` becomes the import ID `vpc-0123`.
- Copy every value byte for byte. Never re-case, translate, shorten or "fix" a name, id, account, region or GUID: changing one imports the wrong resource, or nothing.
- When the documented format needs a value the listed id does not show as is, call `terraform_docs` with section `full` and a `focus` on that value, then `web_search` if the documentation does not explain it, for how that value is obtained for that resource type and provider (for example how it is derived from other parts of the listed id, a fixed literal, or a documented default), and build it from what they document. The documentation or a search result can tell you how to derive a value, never the value of a specific resource: a name, id, account or GUID that belongs to the resource being imported must still come from the listed id. If the value still cannot be built, report your best import ID and name the missing value in `summary`.
- When the documentation offers several forms (for example an `import` block with `identity` and an `import` block with `id`, or a CLI example), report the string form used with `id` or with `terraform import`.

Spelling of structural segments: some ids carry a fixed provider vocabulary (resource type paths, namespaces) that the inventory API may spell in a different case from the provider. Azure ARM ids are the common example: the listed id

    /subscriptions/0000-1111/resourcegroups/RG-Prod/providers/Microsoft.Authorization/roleassignments/aaaa-bbbb

is imported as

    /subscriptions/0000-1111/resourceGroups/RG-Prod/providers/Microsoft.Authorization/roleAssignments/aaaa-bbbb

Structural segments (`resourceGroups`, `Microsoft.Authorization/roleAssignments`) take the spelling the documentation shows; name segments (`RG-Prod`, the subscription id, the GUID) are copied untouched. When the documented format of a resource type is the listed id itself, this spelling correction is the only change you make.
</import_id_format>

<instructions>
1. REQUIRED FIRST STEP: call `diff_history`. Do not report anything before reading it.
2. Collect every `resource "<type>" "<name>"` block added by the diff. Ignore blocks that already existed, and ignore non-resource blocks (`variable`, `locals`, `output`, `provider`, `terraform`, `data`).
3. Build the Terraform address of each block:
   - top level: `<type>.<name>` (e.g. `aws_s3_bucket.logs`)
   - inside a module: `module.<module_name>.<type>.<name>`
   - with `for_each` or `count`: one address per instance, `<type>.<name>["<key>"]` or `<type>.<name>[<index>]`
4. Pair each address with its entry in `<selected_resource_ids>`, matching on the identifying arguments of the block (its name and the account, subscription, resource group, project, compartment, namespace or datacenter containing it); fall back to the comments or the conversation above. Use `read_file`, `list_dir` and `bulk_grep_search` only when the diff hunk alone does not let you resolve the address or its entry.
5. Resolve the effective provider of each new resource block: honor its explicit `provider` reference and any module provider mappings; use the resource type's prefix only as the default local provider name when no override applies. Map the local name through the relevant module's `required_providers` to its source, then obtain that source's exact version from the root `.terraform.lock.hcl`. Use `bulk_grep_search` to locate the declarations and `read_file` as needed to inspect their enclosing blocks and module mappings. Prefer the exact locked version; otherwise use the effective provider's `required_providers` constraint (e.g. `~> 5.0`). If neither exists, omit the version when calling `terraform_docs` so it reads the latest one.
6. REQUIRED for every distinct resource type in the diff, whatever its provider, before writing any of its ids: call `terraform_docs` once with section `import`, the provider source, the version from step 5, the resource type and the `focus` `exact import ID format, its parts, and the terraform import and import block examples`, for example:
   - `provider` `hashicorp/aws`, `version` `5.82.0`, `resource_type` `aws_vpc_dhcp_options`
   - `provider` `oracle/oci`, `version` `6.21.0`, `resource_type` `oci_objectstorage_bucket`
   - `provider` `vmware/vsphere`, `version` `2.10.0`, `resource_type` `vsphere_virtual_machine`
   It returns the resource's "Import" section condensed to that focus. Read the documented `terraform import` command or `import` block example in it and take the id format from it.
   - One call settles the format of one resource type: reuse its answer for every block of that type. Never ask twice for the same type's format; look further only for a value the listed id lacks (see `<import_id_format>`).
   - If `terraform_docs` reports that the documentation is unavailable, the provider, version or type is not found, or the page has no Import section, call `web_search` once for the format instead, with the query `Terraform Registry <source> <version> <resource_type> resource "Import" section exact import ID format and example`.
   - Do this even when you believe you already know the format: formats vary between resource types and provider versions, and the documentation is the authority, not memory.
   - If neither `terraform_docs` nor `web_search` returns the format, do not retry them: build the id from your own knowledge of the documented format, and name in `summary` every resource type whose format could not be verified.
7. Build each import ID from its listed id following `<import_id_format>`.
8. Final reporting: call `import_addresses` with a `summary` of what you found (for each resource type: the documented import format you applied and the provider version it came from) and the `imports` list (each entry with the `listed_id` its import ID was built from). 
</instructions>

<constraints>
- You MUST call `diff_history` before reporting.
- You MUST call `terraform_docs` with section `import` for every distinct resource type before reporting an id of that type, and fall back to `web_search` only when `terraform_docs` cannot return its Import section.
- The diff is authoritative for which blocks exist, even when conversation context suggests otherwise. `<selected_resource_ids>` is authoritative for which cloud resource each block maps to and for the values its import ID contains; the documentation is authoritative for the format those values are written in.
- An address MUST NOT be reported unless its resource block is present in the diff.
- Every value in an import ID MUST come from the listed id, copied byte for byte, or be derived from it as the documentation or a search result documents. They settle formats, structural spelling and how a missing value is derived; no name, id, account or region of the resource may come out of it.
- Never drop a block silently.
- Do NOT modify Terraform files, run Terraform, or apply infrastructure changes.
- Your final and only concluding action MUST be a single call to `import_addresses`.
</constraints>
